POSTS = [
dict(slug="private-limited-vs-llp-vs-opc", cat="Company", date="2026-11-20",
 title="Private Limited vs LLP vs OPC: which one should you register?",
 desc="A plain-English comparison of Private Limited Company, LLP and One Person Company: owners, compliance, tax, funding and cost, so you can pick the right structure.",
 related=["company-registration", "llp-partnership", "one-person-company-registration"],
 html="""<p>Most founders in India choose between three structures: a <b>Private Limited Company</b>, a <b>Limited Liability Partnership (LLP)</b> or a <b>One Person Company (OPC)</b>. All three give you limited liability, which means your personal assets are protected if the business runs into debt. The differences are in who can own it, how much compliance it needs and how investors see it.</p>
<h2>Private Limited Company</h2>
<p>Needs at least two directors and two shareholders (they can be the same people). There is no minimum capital. It is the only structure that can issue shares to angel investors and venture capital funds, and it is the structure most banks and large clients trust.</p>
<ul><li><b>Best for:</b> startups planning to raise funds, hire employees with stock options or scale fast.</li><li><b>Compliance:</b> board meetings, statutory audit every year, annual ROC filings (AOC-4 and MGT-7) and income tax return.</li></ul>
<h2>LLP</h2>
<p>Needs at least two designated partners. Ownership is through the LLP agreement rather than shares. Compliance is lighter: an audit is needed only above a turnover or contribution limit, and the annual filings are Form 11 and Form 8.</p>
<ul><li><b>Best for:</b> professional firms, consultancies and family businesses that do not plan to raise equity.</li><li><b>Limitation:</b> investors usually cannot buy equity in an LLP.</li></ul>
<h2>One Person Company</h2>
<p>A company with a single owner, who must be an Indian citizen resident in India, plus a nominee. It has the credibility of a company with only one founder. It can be converted into a Private Limited Company later as the business grows.</p>
<h2>Quick comparison</h2>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Pvt Ltd</th><th>LLP</th><th>OPC</th></tr><tr><td>Minimum owners</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Raise equity funding</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Convert first</td></tr><tr><td>Statutory audit</td><td>Always</td><td>Above limits</td><td>Always</td></tr><tr><td>Compliance effort</td><td>Higher</td><td>Lower</td><td>Medium</td></tr></table>
<h2>How to decide</h2>
<p>If you plan to raise money or give ESOPs, choose a Private Limited Company. If you are two or more partners running a services business with no funding plans, an LLP keeps costs down. If you are a solo founder who wants a company from day one, start with an OPC.</p>
<p>Not sure? Talk to us on WhatsApp and an expert will recommend the right structure for your plans in a few minutes.</p>"""),

dict(slug="gst-registration-who-needs-it", cat="GST", date="2026-11-20",
 title="Who needs GST registration? Turnover limits and cases where it is compulsory",
 desc="Find out when GST registration is mandatory in India: turnover thresholds, interstate sales, e-commerce sellers and other compulsory cases, plus the documents you need.",
 related=["gst-registration", "gst-return-filing"],
 html="""<p>GST registration gives your business a 15-digit GSTIN. Once registered you must charge GST on your sales, file returns and you can claim input tax credit on your purchases. Here is when you need it.</p>
<h2>Turnover thresholds</h2>
<ul><li><b>Goods:</b> aggregate turnover above ₹40 lakh in a financial year (₹20 lakh in special category states).</li><li><b>Services:</b> aggregate turnover above ₹20 lakh (₹10 lakh in special category states).</li></ul>
<p>Aggregate turnover is all taxable, exempt and export sales across India under the same PAN.</p>
<h2>Compulsory registration, whatever your turnover</h2>
<ul><li>Selling goods from one state to another (interstate supply of goods).</li><li>Selling through e-commerce marketplaces like Amazon or Flipkart.</li><li>Casual taxable persons and non-resident taxable persons.</li><li>Businesses liable to pay tax under reverse charge.</li><li>Input service distributors and agents supplying on behalf of others.</li></ul>
<h2>Voluntary registration</h2>
<p>Many small businesses register even below the limit. Large clients often want a GST invoice, and registration lets you claim credit for GST paid on purchases.</p>
<h2>Documents needed</h2>
<ul><li>PAN and Aadhaar of the proprietor, partners or directors.</li><li>Photograph and contact details.</li><li>Proof of business address: rent agreement or electricity bill, plus an NOC from the owner.</li><li>Bank account details: cancelled cheque or bank statement.</li><li>For a company or LLP: certificate of incorporation and authorisation letter.</li></ul>
<h2>After registration</h2>
<p>You must file returns (GSTR-1 and GSTR-3B) even when there are no sales. Missing returns attracts late fees and can lead to cancellation of your GSTIN. Our plans start at ₹500 a month for nil returns.</p>"""),

dict(slug="trademark-registration-process-india", cat="Trademark", date="2026-11-20",
 title="Trademark registration in India: step-by-step process, fees and timeline",
 desc="How trademark registration works in India: search, filing, examination, objections, publication and registration, with government fees and realistic timelines.",
 related=["trademark-registration", "trademark-objection-reply", "trademark-renewal"],
 html="""<p>A registered trademark gives you the exclusive right to use your brand name or logo for your goods or services across India, and lets you use the ® symbol. Here is how the process works.</p>
<h2>1. Trademark search</h2>
<p>Before filing, check the Trade Marks Registry database for identical or similar marks in your class. A careful search avoids objections and oppositions later.</p>
<h2>2. Choose the class</h2>
<p>Goods and services are divided into 45 classes. You pay a separate government fee for each class you file in, so pick the classes that match what you actually sell.</p>
<h2>3. File the application (Form TM-A)</h2>
<p>The government fee is <b>₹4,500 per class</b> for individuals, startups and small enterprises and <b>₹9,000 per class</b> for others, when filed online. Once filed, you can use the ™ symbol.</p>
<h2>4. Examination</h2>
<p>An examiner checks the application. If there is an objection (for example, the mark is descriptive or similar to an existing one), you must file a reply within the time given, usually 30 days. A strong reply with evidence of use often clears it.</p>
<h2>5. Publication in the Trade Marks Journal</h2>
<p>Accepted marks are published for four months so that anyone can oppose them. If nobody opposes, the mark moves to registration.</p>
<h2>6. Registration</h2>
<p>The registration certificate is issued and the mark is protected for 10 years from the filing date. It can be renewed every 10 years.</p>
<h2>How long does it take?</h2>
<p>Filing takes a day or two. Without objections or oppositions, registration usually takes 8 to 18 months. Your protection starts from the filing date, so file early.</p>"""),

dict(slug="fssai-registration-vs-state-vs-central-licence", cat="Licences", date="2026-11-20",
 title="FSSAI basic registration vs state licence vs central licence",
 desc="Which FSSAI licence does your food business need? Turnover limits for basic registration, state licence and central licence, documents and renewal.",
 related=["fssai-registration", "fssai-renewal", "fssai-annual-return-filing"],
 html="""<p>Every food business in India, from a home kitchen to a factory, needs an FSSAI registration or licence. Which one you need depends mainly on your annual turnover and the type of business.</p>
<h2>Basic FSSAI registration</h2>
<p>For small food businesses with turnover up to <b>₹12 lakh</b> a year: home bakers, small eateries, petty retailers, hawkers and small manufacturers. The government fee is ₹100 per year.</p>
<h2>State FSSAI licence</h2>
<p>For businesses with turnover between <b>₹12 lakh and ₹20 crore</b>: restaurants, caterers, distributors, mid-sized manufacturers and storage units operating within one state.</p>
<h2>Central FSSAI licence</h2>
<p>For businesses with turnover above <b>₹20 crore</b>, importers and exporters, operations in more than one state, and certain categories like e-commerce food businesses and units in airports or seaports.</p>
<h2>Documents usually needed</h2>
<ul><li>Photo ID and address proof of the owner.</li><li>Proof of possession of the premises.</li><li>List of food products.</li><li>For licences: layout of the unit, list of equipment and water test report, depending on the category.</li></ul>
<h2>Validity and renewal</h2>
<p>You can choose a validity of 1 to 5 years. Renew before expiry; operating with an expired licence can lead to penalties and closure orders.</p>
<p>Not sure which one applies? Tell us your business and turnover on WhatsApp and we will tell you exactly what to apply for.</p>"""),

dict(slug="huid-hallmarking-registration-for-jewellers", cat="Licences", date="2026-11-20",
 title="BIS hallmarking and HUID: what every jeweller must know",
 desc="Mandatory gold hallmarking and HUID explained for jewellers: who must register with BIS, the process, documents and what happens if you sell unhallmarked jewellery.",
 related=["bis-certificate"],
 html="""<p>Hallmarking certifies the purity of gold jewellery. In India it is mandatory in notified districts, and every hallmarked piece carries a 6-digit <b>HUID</b> (Hallmark Unique Identification) code.</p>
<h2>Who needs BIS registration?</h2>
<p>Any jeweller who sells gold jewellery or artefacts in a district where hallmarking is mandatory must register with the Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS). Registration is one-time and has no renewal fee in most cases.</p>
<h2>What hallmarked jewellery shows</h2>
<ul><li>The BIS logo.</li><li>The purity in carat and fineness, for example 22K916.</li><li>The 6-digit alphanumeric HUID.</li></ul>
<p>Customers can check any HUID in the BIS CARE app.</p>
<h2>Registration process</h2>
<ol><li>Apply online on the BIS portal with business details.</li><li>Upload documents: GST registration, PAN, address proof of the shop and ID of the owner.</li><li>Pay the registration fee.</li><li>BIS reviews and grants registration, usually within a couple of weeks.</li></ol>
<h2>After registration</h2>
<p>Send your jewellery to a BIS-recognised Assaying and Hallmarking Centre (AHC). They test it, mark it and generate the HUID. You must sell only hallmarked jewellery and keep records as required.</p>
<h2>Penalty</h2>
<p>Selling non-hallmarked gold jewellery where it is mandatory can lead to heavy fines and action under the BIS Act. Registering is quick and inexpensive in comparison.</p>"""),

dict(slug="iec-code-for-import-export", cat="Licences", date="2026-11-20",
 title="IEC code: how to start importing or exporting from India",
 desc="Everything about the Importer Exporter Code (IEC): who needs it, documents, DGFT fee, timeline and the yearly update requirement.",
 related=["online-iec-code-import-export-license", "gst-lut-filing"],
 html="""<p>The <b>Importer Exporter Code (IEC)</b> is a 10-digit code issued by the Directorate General of Foreign Trade (DGFT). You need it to import or export goods, and for most export of services where you claim benefits.</p>
<h2>Who needs IEC?</h2>
<ul><li>Anyone importing goods into India for business.</li><li>Anyone exporting goods out of India.</li><li>Service exporters who want export incentives.</li></ul>
<p>Individuals importing for personal use do not need it.</p>
<h2>Documents</h2>
<ul><li>PAN of the business (or the proprietor).</li><li>Aadhaar or other ID of the applicant.</li><li>Address proof of the business.</li><li>Bank details: cancelled cheque or bank certificate.</li></ul>
<h2>Fee and timeline</h2>
<p>The DGFT fee is <b>₹500</b>. The application is online and the IEC is usually issued within 1 to 3 working days.</p>
<h2>Keep it active</h2>
<p>Your IEC must be updated on the DGFT portal every year between April and June, even if nothing changed. An IEC that is not updated gets deactivated until you update it.</p>
<h2>Exporting? Also file an LUT</h2>
<p>If you are GST registered and export without paying IGST, file a Letter of Undertaking (LUT) every financial year. Without it, you must pay IGST and claim a refund later.</p>"""),

dict(slug="gst-return-due-dates-and-late-fees", cat="GST", date="2026-11-20",
 title="GST return filing: GSTR-1, GSTR-3B, due dates and late fees",
 desc="A simple guide to monthly and quarterly GST returns, QRMP scheme, due dates, late fees and interest, and what happens if you stop filing.",
 related=["gst-return-filing", "gstr-9-annual-filing", "online-gst-nil-return-filing"],
 html="""<p>Once you have a GSTIN, you must file GST returns on time, every period, even when you have no sales. Here are the returns most small businesses file.</p>
<h2>GSTR-1: details of sales</h2>
<p>Lists your outward supplies (invoices). Monthly filers file by the 11th of the next month. Under the QRMP scheme, businesses with turnover up to ₹5 crore can file quarterly.</p>
<h2>GSTR-3B: summary and tax payment</h2>
<p>A summary return in which you pay the net GST after input tax credit. It is generally due on the 20th of the next month for monthly filers. Quarterly filers under QRMP pay tax monthly and file 3B quarterly, with due dates on the 22nd or 24th depending on the state.</p>
<h2>Late fees and interest</h2>
<ul><li>Late fee is charged per day of delay, with a lower amount for nil returns, subject to a maximum.</li><li>Interest at 18% a year is charged on tax paid late.</li></ul>
<h2>What happens if you stop filing?</h2>
<p>Your customers cannot claim credit for invoices you did not report, so they may stop buying from you. After continued non-filing, the GST department can cancel your registration.</p>
<h2>Annual return</h2>
<p>Businesses above the notified turnover limit also file GSTR-9 every year, and larger businesses file GSTR-9C.</p>
<p>Our GST return plans: nil return ₹500, basic ₹999 and advance ₹1,499 a month. We remind you of due dates on WhatsApp.</p>"""),

dict(slug="udyam-msme-registration-benefits", cat="Company", date="2026-11-20",
 title="Udyam (MSME) registration: benefits and how to apply",
 desc="What Udyam registration is, who qualifies as micro, small or medium enterprise, the benefits like priority lending and faster payments, and how to register.",
 related=["business-registration-license", "gst-registration"],
 html="""<p><b>Udyam registration</b> is the government's registration for micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs). It is online, paperless and has no government fee.</p>
<h2>Who qualifies?</h2>
<p>Classification is based on investment in plant and machinery (or equipment) and annual turnover, as notified by the Ministry of MSME. Both manufacturing and service businesses can register: proprietorships, partnerships, LLPs and companies.</p>
<h2>Benefits</h2>
<ul><li>Priority sector lending and collateral-free loans under government schemes.</li><li>Protection against delayed payments: buyers must pay MSMEs within the time set by law, and you can approach the MSME Samadhaan portal.</li><li>Concessions in some government tenders.</li><li>Eligibility for subsidies and schemes run by central and state governments.</li><li>Lower trademark fees for MSMEs.</li></ul>
<h2>What you need</h2>
<ul><li>Aadhaar of the proprietor, managing partner or director.</li><li>PAN and GSTIN of the business (where applicable).</li><li>Bank details, business activity (NIC code), investment and employee numbers.</li></ul>
<h2>How long does it take?</h2>
<p>The Udyam certificate is usually issued within one or two working days. Our fee is ₹499.</p>"""),

dict(slug="annual-compliance-private-limited-company", cat="Compliance", date="2026-11-20",
 title="Annual compliance checklist for a Private Limited Company",
 desc="The yearly compliance every Private Limited Company in India must complete: board meetings, AGM, audit, ROC forms AOC-4 and MGT-7, DIR-3 KYC and ITR.",
 related=["online-annual-compliance-filing", "annual-compliance-bookkeeping", "corporate-compliance-governance"],
 html="""<p>Registering a company is the start. To stay in good standing, every Private Limited Company must complete these filings every year, even with zero turnover.</p>
<h2>Within the year</h2>
<ul><li><b>Board meetings:</b> at least four a year, with a gap of not more than 120 days between two meetings (small companies and OPCs have relaxations).</li><li><b>Statutory registers and minutes:</b> kept up to date.</li><li><b>Auditor:</b> the first auditor is appointed within 30 days of incorporation.</li></ul>
<h2>After the financial year ends (31 March)</h2>
<ul><li><b>Statutory audit</b> of the accounts.</li><li><b>Annual General Meeting (AGM)</b> by 30 September.</li><li><b>AOC-4:</b> financial statements, within 30 days of the AGM.</li><li><b>MGT-7 or MGT-7A:</b> annual return, within 60 days of the AGM.</li><li><b>Income tax return:</b> by the due date for companies.</li><li><b>DIR-3 KYC:</b> every director, by 30 September.</li></ul>
<h2>Penalties</h2>
<p>ROC forms filed late attract an additional fee of ₹100 per day per form, with no upper cap. Long delays can lead to the company being struck off and directors being disqualified.</p>
<h2>Make it effortless</h2>
<p>Our Legal Buddy plan handles corporate compliance and governance for ₹20,000 a month or ₹1,00,000 a year, with reminders and filings handled for you.</p>"""),

dict(slug="sending-a-legal-notice-in-india", cat="Legal", date="2026-11-20",
 title="How to send a legal notice in India (and what to do if you receive one)",
 desc="When to send a legal notice for unpaid dues, cheque bounce, property or consumer disputes, what it must contain, timelines, and how to reply to one.",
 related=["legal-notice", "consumer-complaint", "online-dispute-resolution"],
 html="""<p>A legal notice is a formal letter, usually sent through an advocate, that tells the other party about your claim and asks them to act within a set time. It often settles a dispute without going to court.</p>
<h2>Common reasons to send one</h2>
<ul><li>Unpaid invoices or recovery of money.</li><li>Cheque bounce: under Section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, the notice must be sent within 30 days of learning that the cheque bounced.</li><li>Property, rent and tenancy disputes.</li><li>Consumer complaints about defective goods or poor service.</li><li>Employment disputes and breach of contract.</li></ul>
<h2>What a legal notice contains</h2>
<ul><li>Names and addresses of both parties.</li><li>The facts of the dispute in order.</li><li>The legal basis of the claim.</li><li>What you want: payment, action or compensation.</li><li>A deadline to comply, commonly 15 to 30 days.</li></ul>
<h2>How it is sent</h2>
<p>By registered post or speed post with acknowledgement, and often also by email or WhatsApp. Keep the postal receipt and tracking proof.</p>
<h2>If you receive a legal notice</h2>
<p>Do not ignore it. Note the deadline, collect your documents and send a considered reply through an advocate. A good reply can end the matter or protect your position if it goes to court.</p>
<p>Legal Wegal drafts and sends legal notices and replies through qualified advocates. Your first call is free.</p>"""),
]
